> Module Filing_status

> Using Types
> Using Filers as F
> Using Hoh_qss_persons as H

# Filing status: eligibility, validity of the chosen status, recommendation

Direct File module `filingStatus.xml` ("DF" below,
`../direct-file/direct-file/backend/src/main/resources/tax/filingStatus.xml`,
1,116 lines). Issue #20, scope.md sections 4.1 and 5.4, CONVENTIONS section
7 (stage 3). Tests: `catala/tests/test_filing_status.catala_en`. Oracle
comparison: `difftest/mappings/filing_status.mjs` (`make difftest
SCOPE=filing_status`, over `oracle/facts.csv`, and `make
difftest-filing-status-graphs`, over the 27 filing-status fact graphs of
`direct-file/docs/testing/fact-graphs`, `oracle/fact_graphs.csv`).

Already elsewhere, and read here:

- `Filers` (#15, `filers.xml` plus the marital part of `filingStatus.xml`):
  `/maritalStatusAllowsFilingMarried` (`eligible_for_mfs`),
  `/eligibleForMFJ`, `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh`, `/filerCouldQualifyForQss`,
  `/treatAsMFJ`, `/treatAsMFS`.
- `Hoh_qss_persons` (#19): the household counts of HoH and QSS qualifying
  persons, `Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson)` and
  `Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson)`.

**The filing status is an input** (`/filingStatus` is a `Writable`,
`filingStatus.xml` line 338; CONVENTIONS section 7). There is no cycle: the
person tests read the chosen status only through `/treatAsMFJ`, which needs
only marital facts, and this module reads the counts. DF's UI offers only the
eligible statuses (`/filingStatusOptions`, line 5); here an ineligible choice
is the knockout `ko_filing_status_not_eligible` so that the function stays
total, and `is_filing_status_valid` (DF `/isFilingStatusValid`, line 916)
says the chosen status is among the eligible ones.

Not modelled (CONVENTIONS section 2): the questionnaire facts
(`/filingStatusChoice`, `/filingStatusInitialOptions`, `/wantsJointReturn`
before the status is chosen, `/tpSelectedStatusInSpouseSection...`,
`/statusSelectedInSpouseSection`, `/flowShowFilingStatusChoiceA/B`, the
`/show...FilingStatusScreen` facts, `/hasValid...` and `/hasInvalid...`
screen switches), the capitalised display copies, `/isFilingStatusComplete`
(inputs are complete), and the PDF and MeF helpers (`/xmlQualifyingHOHPerson`,
`/mfsSpouseOrQSSAndHOHNotDependentChild`,
`/marriedAndLivedApartSecondaryFilerResidencyEligible`, `/pdfSpouseTin`).

Where DF disagrees with the statute, the statute wins (CONVENTIONS section
8). This module adds no new divergence: `eligible_for_single` follows DF by
decision (section on IRC 1 below). DF-BUG-19 (issue #19) reaches this module
through the HoH count.

## Declarations: the eligibility check (stage 3)

`FilingStatusEligibility` is the decision itself, with the stage-1 and
stage-2 results as plain inputs. It is small on purpose: it is the part the
Lean side states its filing-status lemma about (scope.md section 5.4:
`unsupported = false` implies the chosen status is eligible).

```catala-metadata
declaration scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # DF: /filingStatus (Writable; CONVENTIONS section 7)
  input filing_status content Types.FilingStatus
  # DF: /maritalStatusAllowsFilingMarried (Filers.eligible_for_mfs)
  input marital_status_allows_filing_married content boolean
  # DF: /eligibleForMFJ (Filers.eligible_for_mfj)
  input eligible_for_mfj content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForHoh (Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_hoh)
  input filer_could_qualify_for_hoh content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForQss (Filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss)
  input filer_could_qualify_for_qss content boolean
  # DF: Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson)
  # (Hoh_qss_persons.HohQssPersons.hoh_qualifying_people_count)
  input hoh_qualifying_people_count content integer
  # DF: Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson)
  # (Hoh_qss_persons.HohQssPersons.qss_qualifying_people_count)
  input qss_qualifying_people_count content integer

  # DF: /eligibleForMFS
  output eligible_for_mfs condition
  # DF: /eligibleForQss
  output eligible_for_qss condition
  # DF: /eligibleForHoh
  output eligible_for_hoh condition
  # DF: /eligibleForSingle (single stays selectable; see IRC 1 below)
  output eligible_for_single condition

  # DF: /isFilingStatusSingle, /isFilingStatusMFJ, /isFilingStatusMFS,
  # /isFilingStatusHOH, /isFilingStatusQSS
  output is_filing_status_single condition
  output is_filing_status_mfj condition
  output is_filing_status_mfs condition
  output is_filing_status_hoh condition
  output is_filing_status_qss condition
  # DF: /treatAsHoh (the filing status is complete, so the first branch)
  output treat_as_hoh condition
  # DF: /wantsSeparateReturn (the filing status is complete, so
  # /isFilingStatusMFS)
  internal wants_separate_return condition

  # DF: EnumOptionsSize(/filingStatusOptions)
  output eligible_filing_status_count content integer
  # DF: /hasMultipleEligibleFilingStatuses
  output has_multiple_eligible_filing_statuses condition
  # DF: /isFilingStatusValid: the chosen status is among the eligible ones
  output is_filing_status_valid condition
  # DF: /recommendedFilingStatus
  output recommended_filing_status content Types.FilingStatus

  # CONVENTIONS section 7, stage 3: the chosen status is not eligible. No DF
  # fact (DF's UI never offers an ineligible status).
  output ko_filing_status_not_eligible condition
  # CONVENTIONS section 6: every ko_ of this scope
  output unsupported condition
```

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # DF: /isFilingStatusSingle
  definition is_filing_status_single equals filing_status = Single
  # DF: /isFilingStatusMFJ
  definition is_filing_status_mfj equals filing_status = MarriedFilingJointly
  # DF: /isFilingStatusMFS
  definition is_filing_status_mfs equals filing_status = MarriedFilingSeparately
  # DF: /isFilingStatusHOH
  definition is_filing_status_hoh equals filing_status = HeadOfHousehold
  # DF: /isFilingStatusQSS
  definition is_filing_status_qss equals
    filing_status = QualifyingSurvivingSpouse
  # DF: /treatAsHoh
  definition treat_as_hoh equals is_filing_status_hoh
  # DF: /wantsSeparateReturn
  definition wants_separate_return equals is_filing_status_mfs
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 6013 Joint returns of income tax by husband and wife; § 7703 Determination of marital status

### Married filing jointly and separately

IRC 6013(a) (`sources/statute/irc-6013.html`): "A husband and wife may make a
single return jointly of income taxes under subtitle A, even though one of
the spouses has neither gross income nor deductions, except as provided
below: (1) no joint return shall be made if either the husband or wife at any
time during the taxable year is a nonresident alien". IRC 7703(a)
(`sources/statute/irc-7703.html`): "(1) the determination of whether an
individual is married shall be made as of the close of his taxable year;
except that if his spouse dies during his taxable year such determination
shall be made as of the time of such death; and (2) an individual legally
separated from his spouse under a decree of divorce or of separate
maintenance shall not be considered as married." Treas. Reg. § 1.7703-1(a)
(`sources/regs/cfr-1.7703-1.xml`): "an individual shall be considered as
married even though living apart from his spouse unless legally separated
under a decree of divorce or separate maintenance."

Pub. 501 (2024), `sources/pubs/p501--2024.pdf` p 6: "Spouse died during the
year. If your spouse died during the year, you are considered married for the
whole year for filing status purposes. If you didn't remarry before the end
of the tax year, you can file a joint return for yourself and your deceased
spouse." p 7: "You can choose married filing separately as your filing status
if you are married."

These are `Filers` (#15): `/eligibleForMFJ` is "married or widowed in the tax
year" (`/maritalStatusAllowsFilingMarried`, `filingStatus.xml` line 902) and
the spouse's residency (`/secondaryFilerResidencyEligibleForMFJ`, which
includes the 6013(g) election); the filer's own residency is the knockout
`/primaryFilerResidencyKnockout`. `/eligibleForMFS` (line 529) is
`/maritalStatusAllowsFilingMarried`. Pub. 501 p 7 says a married person may
also choose head of household when "considered unmarried", so MFS stays
available next to HoH (IRC 1(d) taxes "every married individual (as defined
in section 7703) who does not make a single return jointly").

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # IRC 1(d), 7703(a); Pub 501 p 7. DF: /eligibleForMFS
  definition eligible_for_mfs equals marital_status_allows_filing_married
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 2 Definitions and special rules

### (a) Definition of surviving spouse

IRC 2(a)(1) (`sources/statute/irc-2.html`): "For purposes of section 1, the
term "surviving spouse" means a taxpayer— (A) whose spouse died during either
of his two taxable years immediately preceding the taxable year, and (B) who
maintains as his home a household which constitutes for the taxable year the
principal place of abode (as a member of such household) of a dependent (i)
who (within the meaning of section 152, determined without regard to
subsections (b)(1), (b)(2), and (d)(1)(B) thereof) is a son, stepson,
daughter, or stepdaughter of the taxpayer, and (ii) with respect to whom the
taxpayer is entitled to a deduction for the taxable year under section 151."
IRC 2(a)(2): "a taxpayer shall not be considered to be a surviving spouse—
(A) if the taxpayer has remarried at any time before the close of the taxable
year, or (B) unless, for the taxpayer's taxable year during which his spouse
died, a joint return could have been made under the provisions of section
6013 (without regard to subsection (a)(3) thereof)."

The filer side, 2(a)(1)(A) and 2(a)(2), is `/filerCouldQualifyForQss`
(`Filers`: widowed in 2022 or 2023, and could have filed jointly in the year
of death; a remarried filer answers "married"). The dependent of 2(a)(1)(B)
is `/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson` (`Hoh_qss_persons`). DF
`/eligibleForQss` (line 576) is both, with "the Count of
`qssQualifyingPerson` is greater than 0".

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # IRC 2(a)(1), (2); Pub 501 pp 10-11. DF: /eligibleForQss
  rule eligible_for_qss under condition
    filer_could_qualify_for_qss and qss_qualifying_people_count > 0
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (b) Definition of head of household

IRC 2(b)(1): "an individual shall be considered a head of a household if,
and only if, such individual is not married at the close of his taxable
year, is not a surviving spouse (as defined in subsection (a)), and either—
(A) maintains as his home a household which constitutes for more than
one-half of such taxable year the principal place of abode, as a member of
such household, of— (i) a qualifying child of the individual ... or (ii) any
other person who is a dependent of the taxpayer ..., or (B) maintains a
household which constitutes for such taxable year the principal place of
abode of the father or mother of the taxpayer". IRC 2(b)(2): "(A) an
individual who is legally separated from his spouse under a decree of divorce
or of separate maintenance shall not be considered as married; (B) a taxpayer
shall be considered as not married at the close of his taxable year if at
any time during the taxable year his spouse is a nonresident alien; and (C) a
taxpayer shall be considered as married at the close of his taxable year if
his spouse (other than a spouse described in subparagraph (B)) died during
the taxable year." IRC 2(c): "For purposes of this part, an individual shall
be treated as not married at the close of the taxable year if such individual
is so treated under the provisions of section 7703(b)." IRC 7703(b): a married
individual who files a separate return, keeps up a home for a child, and
"(3) during the last 6 months of the taxable year, such individual's spouse
is not a member of such household, such individual shall not be considered
as married."

"Not married at the close" is `/filerCouldQualifyForHoh` (`Filers`): not
eligible for a joint return (unmarried, divorced, widowed before the tax
year, or married to a nonresident alien, 2(b)(2)(B)), or eligible for a
joint return and living apart from the spouse the last six months
(7703(b)(3); the child tests of 7703(b)(1)-(2) are applied per person by
`Hoh_qss_persons`, `considered_unmarried_child`). The qualifying person is
`/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson`. "Is not a surviving spouse":
DF `/eligibleForHoh` (line 549) ANDs in `not /eligibleForQss`, with the XML
comment "Filers who are eligible for QSS are not eligible for HOH, preventing
them from taking a less advantageous refund 26 U.S.C. § 2(b)(1)". Treas. Reg.
§ 1.2-2(a)(3) (`sources/regs/cfr-1.2-2.xml`): "If the taxpayer does not
qualify as a surviving spouse he may nevertheless qualify as a head of a
household". So QSS preempts HoH, as in DF.

IRC 2(b)(3)(A) (a filer who is a nonresident alien at any time) is the
knockout `/primaryFilerResidencyKnockout` (`Filers`).

A spouse who died during the tax year: 2(b)(2)(C) makes the filer married.
DF treats such a filer like a married one (eligible for a joint return; head
of household only through `/livedApartLastSixMonths`), which this module
reads as given (open question in the PR of #20).

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # IRC 2(b)(1), (2), 2(c), 7703(b); Treas. Reg. 1.2-2(a)(3), (b)(1); Pub 501
  # pp 8-10. DF: /eligibleForHoh
  rule eligible_for_hoh under condition
    hoh_qualifying_people_count > 0
    and filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
    and not eligible_for_qss
  consequence fulfilled
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 1 Tax imposed: who may use the single rates

IRC 1(c) (`sources/statute/irc-1.html`), "Unmarried individuals (other than
surviving spouses and heads of households)": "There is hereby imposed on the
taxable income of every individual (other than a surviving spouse as defined
in section 2(a) or the head of a household as defined in section 2(b)) who is
not a married individual (as defined in section 7703) a tax determined in
accordance with the following table". IRC 2(b)(1) defines the head of a
household "if, and only if" its conditions hold: there is no election. Pub.
501 (2024) p 6, Single: "Your filing status is single if you are considered
unmarried and you don't qualify for another filing status." And p 6: "Head
of household or qualifying surviving spouse. If you are considered
unmarried, you may be able to file as head of household or as a qualifying
surviving spouse."

DF `/eligibleForSingle` (line 538) is `not
/maritalStatusAllowsFilingMarried`, so DF offers "single" to an unmarried
filer who is also a head of household or a surviving spouse, next to HoH or
QSS. Read strictly, 1(c) does not tax such a filer at the single rates.

**Decision (orchestrator and human, issue #20): single stays a permitted
choice for every unmarried filer, as in DF.** Reasons: the single rates and
standard deduction are never more favourable than HoH or QSS, so choosing
single costs only the filer; the IRS processes such returns and does not
reject them; and the filing status is the filer's answer (CONVENTIONS section
7), not something this spec overrides. So `eligible_for_single` is DF's
definition, a return that chooses single while eligible for HoH or QSS is
valid and not knocked out, and the recommendation (below) still names the
better status: QSS or HoH before single, as Pub. 501 p 6 directs ("you don't
qualify for another filing status"). No DF-BUG is recorded here.

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # Single stays selectable for an unmarried filer even when HoH or QSS is
  # also eligible (decision above): the recommendation names the better
  # status, but a filer may choose the less favourable one. IRC 1(c),
  # 7703(a); Pub 501 p 6. DF: /eligibleForSingle
  definition eligible_for_single equals not marital_status_allows_filing_married
```

## The chosen status and the recommendation

### Validity of the chosen status

DF `/isFilingStatusValid` (line 916): the chosen status is MFJ and
`/eligibleForMFJ`, or MFS and `/eligibleForMFS`, or single and
`/eligibleForSingle`, or HoH and `/eligibleForHoh`, or QSS and
`/eligibleForQss`. `/filingStatusOptions` (line 5) lists the same five
pairs, and `/hasMultipleEligibleFilingStatuses` (line 742) is "more than one
option". CONVENTIONS section 7, stage 3: an ineligible choice is the
knockout `ko_filing_status_not_eligible`.

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # DF: /isFilingStatusValid
  rule is_filing_status_valid under condition
    (is_filing_status_mfj and eligible_for_mfj)
    or (is_filing_status_mfs and eligible_for_mfs)
    or (is_filing_status_single and eligible_for_single)
    or (is_filing_status_hoh and eligible_for_hoh)
    or (is_filing_status_qss and eligible_for_qss)
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: EnumOptionsSize(/filingStatusOptions)
  definition eligible_filing_status_count equals
    (if eligible_for_mfj then 1 else 0)
    + (if eligible_for_mfs then 1 else 0)
    + (if eligible_for_single then 1 else 0)
    + (if eligible_for_hoh then 1 else 0)
    + (if eligible_for_qss then 1 else 0)

  # DF: /hasMultipleEligibleFilingStatuses
  rule has_multiple_eligible_filing_statuses under condition
    eligible_filing_status_count > 1
  consequence fulfilled

  # CONVENTIONS section 7, stage 3
  rule ko_filing_status_not_eligible under condition
    not is_filing_status_valid
  consequence fulfilled

  # CONVENTIONS section 6
  rule unsupported under condition ko_filing_status_not_eligible
  consequence fulfilled
```

### The recommended status

DF `/recommendedFilingStatus` (line 234), "The most advantageous filing status
for the taxpayer.": for a filer eligible for a joint return, MFJ unless the
filer wants a separate return, then HoH if eligible, otherwise MFS; for
everyone else, the first eligible of QSS, HoH, MFS, single. No statute
defines a recommendation; this is DF's rule, reproduced as is. Outside the
joint-return branch the order is the one the law imposes or that always
favours the filer: QSS preempts HoH (2(b)(1)), HoH and QSS come before single
(1(c), Pub. 501 p 6; single stays selectable but is never recommended over
them), and
MFS and single never overlap (`eligible_for_mfs` is "married",
`eligible_for_single` is "not married"). For a married filer who may also
file separately or as HoH, DF picks MFJ without comparing taxes; Pub. 501 p 6
suggests figuring "your tax both on a joint return and on separate returns",
which needs the whole return under each status (left to the caller, PR of
#20).

The recommendation is always eligible: each branch tests the eligibility of
the status it returns, and the last branch is reached only when the filer is
not married (`eligible_for_mfs` false), which is `eligible_for_single`. The test file checks this on every status.

```catala
scope FilingStatusEligibility:
  # Pub 501 p 6 (no statute). DF: /recommendedFilingStatus
  definition recommended_filing_status equals
    if eligible_for_mfj then
      (if not wants_separate_return then MarriedFilingJointly
       else if eligible_for_hoh then HeadOfHousehold
       else MarriedFilingSeparately)
    else if eligible_for_qss then QualifyingSurvivingSpouse
    else if eligible_for_hoh then HeadOfHousehold
    else if eligible_for_mfs then MarriedFilingSeparately
    else Single
```

## The three stages in one scope

`FilingStatusReturn` runs the stages of CONVENTIONS section 7 in order on the
user's answers: `Filers` (stage 1), `Hoh_qss_persons.HohQssPersons` over the
household (stage 2), then `FilingStatusEligibility` (stage 3). It is what the
issue's test cases and the oracle comparison run, and what the return scope
uses to replace its plain filing-status inputs (PR of #20, wiring list).

`Filers` takes `/eligibleForHoh` as an input for one knockout only
(`/knockoutSubjectToCommunityPropertyLaws`). Every other output of `Filers`
does not read it, so `Filers` runs twice: once with `eligible_for_hoh` false,
whose outputs feed stages 2 and 3, and once with the stage-3
`eligible_for_hoh`, read only for `unsupported`. Both calls are pure; there is
no fixed point (CONVENTIONS section 7).

```catala-metadata
declaration scope FilingStatusReturn:
  # DF: /filingStatus (Writable)
  input filing_status content Types.FilingStatus
  # The inputs of Filers.Filers (filers.xml), except /eligibleForHoh, which is
  # computed here
  input marital_status content F.MaritalStatus
  input year_of_spouse_death content F.YearOfSpouseDeath
  input primary_filer content F.FilerAnswers
  input secondary_filer content F.FilerAnswers
  input secondary_filer_date_of_death content date
  input primary_filer_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund content boolean
  input mfj_required_to_file content boolean
  input mfj_dependents_filing_for_credits content boolean
  input lived_together_all_year_with_spouse content boolean
  input spouse_lived_together_months content F.SpouseLivedTogetherMonths
  input writable_lived_apart_last_six_months content boolean
  input writable_separation_agreement content boolean
  input can_file_jointly_year_of_spouse_death content boolean
  input in_registered_domestic_partnership content boolean
  input filer_residence_and_income_state content Types.ScopedState
  input spouse_itemizes content boolean
  input mfs_living_spouse_filing_return content boolean
  input mfs_deceased_spouse_filing_return content boolean
  input mfs_spouse_has_gross_income content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold
  input members content list of H.Member

  # Stage 1 (Filers), with eligible_for_hoh false; see above
  output filers content F.Filers
  # Stage 2: the household
  output household content H.HohQssPersons
  # Stage 3
  output eligibility content FilingStatusEligibility
  # Filers again, with the stage-3 eligible_for_hoh, for its knockouts only
  internal filers_knockouts content F.Filers

  # Scalar copies of the results, for the oracle comparison and the return
  # scope. DF paths in the comments.
  # DF: /maritalStatusAllowsFilingMarried
  output marital_status_allows_filing_married condition
  # DF: /eligibleForMFJ
  output eligible_for_mfj condition
  # DF: /eligibleForMFS
  output eligible_for_mfs condition
  # DF: /eligibleForSingle
  output eligible_for_single condition
  # DF: /eligibleForHoh
  output eligible_for_hoh condition
  # DF: /eligibleForQss
  output eligible_for_qss condition
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForHoh
  output filer_could_qualify_for_hoh condition
  # DF: filers.xml /filerCouldQualifyForQss
  output filer_could_qualify_for_qss condition
  # DF: Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/hohQualifyingPerson)
  output hoh_qualifying_people_count content integer
  # DF: Count(/familyAndHousehold/*/qssQualifyingPerson)
  output qss_qualifying_people_count content integer
  # DF: /isFilingStatusValid
  output is_filing_status_valid condition
  # DF: /hasMultipleEligibleFilingStatuses
  output has_multiple_eligible_filing_statuses condition
  # DF: /recommendedFilingStatus
  output recommended_filing_status content Types.FilingStatus
  # The same, as five flags (the oracle comparison reads scalars only)
  output recommended_is_single condition
  output recommended_is_mfj condition
  output recommended_is_mfs condition
  output recommended_is_hoh condition
  output recommended_is_qss condition
  # DF: /treatAsMFJ, /treatAsMFS, /treatAsHoh
  output treat_as_mfj condition
  output treat_as_mfs condition
  output treat_as_hoh condition
  # Stage 3 knockout
  output ko_filing_status_not_eligible condition
  # CONVENTIONS section 6: this scope's knockout and the sub-scopes'
  output unsupported condition
```

```catala
scope FilingStatusReturn:
  definition filers equals
    output of F.Filers with {
      -- filing_status: filing_status
      -- marital_status: marital_status
      -- year_of_spouse_death: year_of_spouse_death
      -- primary_filer: primary_filer
      -- secondary_filer: secondary_filer
      -- secondary_filer_date_of_death: secondary_filer_date_of_death
      -- primary_filer_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund:
           primary_filer_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund
      -- mfj_required_to_file: mfj_required_to_file
      -- mfj_dependents_filing_for_credits: mfj_dependents_filing_for_credits
      -- lived_together_all_year_with_spouse: lived_together_all_year_with_spouse
      -- spouse_lived_together_months: spouse_lived_together_months
      -- writable_lived_apart_last_six_months: writable_lived_apart_last_six_months
      -- writable_separation_agreement: writable_separation_agreement
      -- can_file_jointly_year_of_spouse_death: can_file_jointly_year_of_spouse_death
      -- in_registered_domestic_partnership: in_registered_domestic_partnership
      -- filer_residence_and_income_state: filer_residence_and_income_state
      -- spouse_itemizes: spouse_itemizes
      -- mfs_living_spouse_filing_return: mfs_living_spouse_filing_return
      -- mfs_deceased_spouse_filing_return: mfs_deceased_spouse_filing_return
      -- mfs_spouse_has_gross_income: mfs_spouse_has_gross_income
      # Read only by the community-property knockout; see above
      -- eligible_for_hoh: false }

  definition household equals
    output of H.HohQssPersons with {
      -- members: members
      -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth
      -- treat_filers_as_dependents: filers.treat_filers_as_dependents
      -- either_filer_us_citizen_or_national:
           filers.either_filer_us_citizen_or_national
      -- treat_as_mfj: filers.treat_as_mfj
      -- eligible_for_mfj: filers.eligible_for_mfj
      -- filer_could_qualify_for_hoh: filers.filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
      -- filer_could_qualify_for_qss: filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss
      -- filing_status: filing_status }

  definition eligibility equals
    output of FilingStatusEligibility with {
      -- filing_status: filing_status
      -- marital_status_allows_filing_married: filers.eligible_for_mfs
      -- eligible_for_mfj: filers.eligible_for_mfj
      -- filer_could_qualify_for_hoh: filers.filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
      -- filer_could_qualify_for_qss: filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss
      -- hoh_qualifying_people_count: household.hoh_qualifying_people_count
      -- qss_qualifying_people_count: household.qss_qualifying_people_count }

  definition filers_knockouts equals
    output of F.Filers with {
      -- filing_status: filing_status
      -- marital_status: marital_status
      -- year_of_spouse_death: year_of_spouse_death
      -- primary_filer: primary_filer
      -- secondary_filer: secondary_filer
      -- secondary_filer_date_of_death: secondary_filer_date_of_death
      -- primary_filer_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund:
           primary_filer_potential_claimer_filed_only_for_refund
      -- mfj_required_to_file: mfj_required_to_file
      -- mfj_dependents_filing_for_credits: mfj_dependents_filing_for_credits
      -- lived_together_all_year_with_spouse: lived_together_all_year_with_spouse
      -- spouse_lived_together_months: spouse_lived_together_months
      -- writable_lived_apart_last_six_months: writable_lived_apart_last_six_months
      -- writable_separation_agreement: writable_separation_agreement
      -- can_file_jointly_year_of_spouse_death: can_file_jointly_year_of_spouse_death
      -- in_registered_domestic_partnership: in_registered_domestic_partnership
      -- filer_residence_and_income_state: filer_residence_and_income_state
      -- spouse_itemizes: spouse_itemizes
      -- mfs_living_spouse_filing_return: mfs_living_spouse_filing_return
      -- mfs_deceased_spouse_filing_return: mfs_deceased_spouse_filing_return
      -- mfs_spouse_has_gross_income: mfs_spouse_has_gross_income
      -- eligible_for_hoh: eligibility.eligible_for_hoh }

  definition marital_status_allows_filing_married equals filers.eligible_for_mfs
  definition eligible_for_mfj equals filers.eligible_for_mfj
  definition eligible_for_mfs equals eligibility.eligible_for_mfs
  definition eligible_for_single equals eligibility.eligible_for_single
  definition eligible_for_hoh equals eligibility.eligible_for_hoh
  definition eligible_for_qss equals eligibility.eligible_for_qss
  definition filer_could_qualify_for_hoh equals filers.filer_could_qualify_for_hoh
  definition filer_could_qualify_for_qss equals filers.filer_could_qualify_for_qss
  definition hoh_qualifying_people_count equals
    household.hoh_qualifying_people_count
  definition qss_qualifying_people_count equals
    household.qss_qualifying_people_count
  definition is_filing_status_valid equals eligibility.is_filing_status_valid
  definition has_multiple_eligible_filing_statuses equals
    eligibility.has_multiple_eligible_filing_statuses
  definition recommended_filing_status equals eligibility.recommended_filing_status
  definition recommended_is_single equals recommended_filing_status = Single
  definition recommended_is_mfj equals
    recommended_filing_status = MarriedFilingJointly
  definition recommended_is_mfs equals
    recommended_filing_status = MarriedFilingSeparately
  definition recommended_is_hoh equals recommended_filing_status = HeadOfHousehold
  definition recommended_is_qss equals
    recommended_filing_status = QualifyingSurvivingSpouse
  definition treat_as_mfj equals filers.treat_as_mfj
  definition treat_as_mfs equals filers.treat_as_mfs
  definition treat_as_hoh equals eligibility.treat_as_hoh
  definition ko_filing_status_not_eligible equals
    eligibility.ko_filing_status_not_eligible

  # CONVENTIONS section 6: this scope's knockout, Filers' knockouts (with the
  # stage-3 eligible_for_hoh) and the household's
  rule unsupported under condition
    ko_filing_status_not_eligible
    or filers_knockouts.unsupported
    or household.unsupported
  consequence fulfilled
```
